# APEX-OS — let the desktop switch into (and out of) Gaming Mode.
#
# The shell's power menu offers "Gaming Mode", which has to record the session
# the greeter should preselect and then end the current session. The record lives
# in /var/lib/apex-greet, which is 0755 greetd:greetd — a session user cannot
# write it, and should not be able to.
#
# NOPASSWD because this is invoked from a compositor-spawned script, which has no
# way to present a password prompt. The user still authenticates at the greeter
# immediately afterwards: APEX-OS deliberately does NOT configure autologin the
# way SteamOS and Bazzite do, because both target machines end up on LUKS2+TPM2
# and a passwordless graphical login is not a change to make silently.
#
# Deliberately narrow. The helper takes no caller-supplied paths and validates
# the session id against the .desktop files actually installed in
# /usr/share/wayland-sessions, so the only strings that can reach the state file
# are names of real shipped sessions. Terminating one's own session is already
# available to any user via loginctl, so the sole privileged effect is that
# validated write.
#
# %wheel, matching the Windows-boot rule: switching the machine's boot-to session
# is an administrative act on shared state, unlike setting your own wallpaper.
%wheel ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/libexec/apex-session-select *
